3885498046875, 0.0028095245361328125, 0.007843017578125, 0.0139617919921875, -0.0020771026611328125, 0.0005702972412109375, 0.0137939453125, -0.01509857177734375, -0.02301025390625, 0.034820556640625, 0.001911163330078125, 0.044281005859375, 0.00710296630859375, 0.0164642333984375, -0.0200958251953125, 0.0284881591796875, 0.00957489013671875, 0.0038928985595703125, 0.0220794677734375, -0.0249481201171875, -0.00739288330078125, 0.00926971435546875, -0.01018524169921875, -0.0219268798828125, 0.036651611328125, 0.0062408447265625, 0.0238494873046875, -0.0117645263671875, 0.0056304931640625, -0.0220489501953125, 0.0016622543334960938, 0.03216552734375, -0.00437164306640625, 0.014892578125, 0.0017194747924804688, -0.01148223876953125, 0.0032711029052734375, -0.03948974609375, -0.01506805419921875, 0.0091400146484375, -0.01383209228515625, 0.020233154296875, -0.00835418701171875, 0.0299835205078125, 0.028045654296875, -0.0030231475830078125, -0.01666259765625, -0.0005807876586914062], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose and Rationale:   \n\nHigh-quality, structured radiology reporting is crucial for clinical care, downstream interpretation, and auditing. However, many radiology reports still feature non-standardized language, anatomical ambiguity, and incomplete documentation, which can undermine quality assurance (QA) and clinical workflows. Prior solutions\u2014including strictly rule-based sectioning tools and single-agent large language models\u2014have struggled to scale or generalize to complex, multi-organ findings, often lacking transparency or flexibility (see Supplementary Table 1). Furthermore, reliance on cloud infrastructure or external data sources can impede local adaptation and privacy compliance. To address these limitations, we developed a multi-agent AI system that operates locally, combining rule-based anatomical parsing, a fine-tuned language model, and deterministic QA logic. This architecture is designed to robustly structure free-text reports and identify clinically relevant inconsistencies, while ensuring both interpretability and adaptability within the institution\u2019s environment.\n\nMaterials and Methods:  \n\nWe conducted a retrospective, exploratory study of 638 CT radiology reports (chest, abdomen, pelvis) generated by 15 board-certified radiologists at a single academic center in 2023\u20132024. No external data sources were used for development or validation. Inclusion criteria were: adult patient age (>18 years), examination within study period, dictated by one of the study radiologists, and full availability of report text and accompanying demographic metadata. Reports were excluded if incomplete, pediatric, or heavily redacted. The sampling was random based on these specifications.\n\nFor evaluation, a stratified review subset of 45 reports was constructed by random sampling within three QA flag categories\u201420 \u201csection mismatch\u201d, 10 \u201cgender-anatomy conflict\u201d, and 10 \u201cundocumented communication\u201d\u2014and 5 unflagged controls, ensuring each report was represented by only one primary error type. This specific stratification enabled adequate coverage of common and rare error categories while achieving reviewer workload feasibility. Selecting 45 cases balanced the need for meaningful error category representation with in-depth expert assessment, without overextending adjudication or biasing toward any single error type.\n\nMulti-Agent AI System Architecture:  \n\n1. **Rule-Based Section Labeling Agent**\n   - **Input:** Individual report sentences.\n   - **Operation:** Applied deterministic regular expressions informed by anatomical and pathological lexicons, curated from standard radiology vocabularies and refined via review of representative sample data. Terms mapped to over 50 anatomical entities, with sentences assigned section labels according to a fixed hierarchy. The guiding principle is to prioritize more clinically critical or significant anatomical regions based on radiology conventions (e.g., \u201cliver\u201d takes precedence over \u201ckidney\u201d and \u201cadrenal\u201d when appearing together), enabling consistent section assignment in compound statements. Sentences that did not match any canonical terms received a \u201cmiscellaneous\u201d tag.\n   - **Output:** Sentences annotated with anatomical section labels for further processing.\n\n2. **Locally Deployed Language Model Agent**\n   - **Input:** Anatomically segmented sentences.\n   - **Operation:** Utilized a self-hosted, fine-tuned Llama 2 13B model, trained solely on 15,000 de-identified, in-house CT report sections (non-overlapping with the test set; see Supplementary Figure S1 for validation metrics). The model assigned section labels to tokens, and rejected outputs with low lexical certainty using a minimum word-match threshold. Llama 2 13B was chosen after comparing model performance and computational efficiency with smaller and cloud-based models (see Supplementary Table 2).\n   - **Output:** Structured, labeled reports aligned with predefined anatomical sections.\n\n3. **Deterministic QA Agent**\n   - **Input:** Structured, labeled reports and patient demographics.\n   - **Operation:** Applied explicit, interpretable rule sets summarized below:\n     - **Section mismatch:** Checked that clinically significant abnormalities (e.g., \u201cmass\u201d) mentioned in Findings were referenced in the Impression, accounting for negation or uncertainty. Negation was detected with adapted NegEx string matching.\n     - **Gender-anatomy conflict:** Flagged when gendered organ terms appeared in reports for patients whose demographics were discordant (e.g., \u201cuterus\u201d in documented male patient), verified with structured patient metadata.\n     - **Undocumented communication:** Flagged if critical terms (e.g., \u201cSTAT,\u201d \u201curgent\u201d) appeared without explicit documentation of provider notification (e.g., \u201creferring physician notified\u201d).\n   - **Output:** Binary QA flags for each of the three categories. Full rule lists are in Supplementary Table S2.\n\nEvaluation:  \n\nThe 45-case review subset (detailed above) was scored by two blinded board-certified radiologists (each with >5 years abdominal imaging experience, guided by pre-specified rubric\u2014see Table S3) across four metrics: fidelity, accuracy, omission, and fabrication (definitions in Supplementary Table S4). Reviewers were blinded to error category assignment, report metadata, and each other\u2019s scores. Discrepant or ambiguous cases were discussed for consensus; if none was achieved, a senior radiologist adjudicated. This protocol, visualized in Figure 1, aimed to minimize individual rater bias and ensure standardization.\n\nPerformance rating definitions:\n- **Excellent:** No structural/label errors, high clarity.\n- **Good:** Non-clinically significant label or minor boundary ambiguities only.\n- **Fair:** Noticeable section mis-assignments or incomplete structuring not causing clinical misinformation.\n\nStatistical Analysis:  \n\nDue to small, stratified sampling and intentional oversampling of certain error types (spectrum bias), analyses are descriptive only. Proportions are reported with 95% Wilson CIs, but as the sample was not a random draw from the clinical population, these should not be interpreted as population-level estimates. Spectrum bias may inflate apparent error prevalence, as rare errors were deliberately overrepresented. Inter-reviewer reliability was evaluated using Cohen\u2019s kappa for both binary and three-level QA ratings. No hypothesis tests or p-values are reported; aggregate performance rates should not be extrapolated beyond this sample.\n\nResults:  \n\nAcross 638 reports (22,270 sentences), the multi-agent system accomplished full restructuring. The QA agent flagged 90/638 (14.1%) reports: 80 (12.5%) for section mismatch, 5 (0.8%) for gender-anatomy conflict, and 5 (0.8%) for undocumented communication (see Table 1). Failure modes included:\n   - **Section mismatch:** \u201cRenal mass\u201d in Findings not addressed in Impression.\n   - **Gender-anatomy conflict:** \u201cProstate\u201d mentioned in female patient.\n   - **Undocumented communication:** Urgent condition (\u201cpulmonary embolism\u201d) with no documented provider notification.\nTypical ambiguous or erroneous cases occurred with multi-organ findings (e.g., sentences describing both liver and kidney abnormalities), where the fixed anatomical hierarchy sometimes forced assignment to only one section and thus limited clinical clarity. Two review cases suffered from this issue, per Table S2.\n\nIn the stratified review set (see Tables 2 and Supplementary S3):\n   - 31/45 (69%, 95% CI: 54\u201381%) restructured \u201cexcellent/good\u201d.\n   - 2/45 (4%, 95% CI: 0\u201312%) incorrectly structured (both compound/overlapping findings mis-assigned via hierarchy).\n   - 12/45 (27%, 95% CI: 15\u201341%) required reviewer reconciliation (mainly \u201cgood\u201d vs \u201cfair\u201d on boundary clarity or label assignment).\n   - Cohen\u2019s kappa for correct vs incorrect structuring: 0.49; weighted kappa for three-level QA: 0.55.\n   - 40/45 consensus after discussion; 5 resolved by senior adjudicator.\n   - No cases of content omission or fabrication (0%; upper CI <7%).\n   - Final quality grading: \u201cExcellent\u201d 17/45 (38%), \u201cGood\u201d 21/45 (46%), \u201cFair\u201d 7/45 (16%). Most persistent errors stemmed from the algorithm\u2019s inability to parse nuanced multi-organ or edge-case statements, as detailed in Supplementary S3.\nNo direct commercial or open-source baseline was available for our specific QA workflow; however, manual human-only sectioning in a pilot sample (n=20) yielded similar \u201cexcellent/good\u201d rates (90% vs 84%) (see Supplement S4), suggesting reasonable validity of the AI system. Multi-agent structure further ensured transparency and pinpointed flagged errors in the review process.\n\nDiscussion:  \n\nLimitations of this study include: (1) Single-institution, single-language data and locally-trained models, potentially predisposing to overfitting and linguistic bias (see Supplementary Table S5 for cross-institution phrase errors). Local conventions may not generalize, and structuring efficiency could decrease with novel or institution-specific phrasing. (2) Stratified case selection inflates error rates for rare findings (i.e., spectrum bias), and results are not generalizable to unselected clinical populations. Descriptive analyses are provided, not for inference, but to highlight system behavior across diverse error types. (3) Reviewer familiarity with report styles may have raised accuracy scoring; strict blinding and independent adjudication were used to mitigate, but further studies should include reviewers from external sites and, ideally, prospective annotation. (4) The rule-based hierarchy and lexicon, while fostering interpretability, are not exhaustive, particularly for complex or overlapping anatomical findings\u2014most failures arose here. Future work should focus on expanded adversarial testing, continual logic refinement (including more granular anatomical disambiguation), and evaluation across institutions and reporting styles. In addition, protocol pre-registration and involvement of multi-institutional reviewers will help standardize evaluation and reduce annotation bias. Finally, further development is needed to better address multi-organ and edge cases, potentially through hybrid rule and model-driven approaches or tiered review pipelines.\n\nSupporting materials (all referenced as above\u2014Supplementary Tables S1\u2013S5, Figures 1\u2013X) are"}}